ement: less likely given acute onset and blasts in CBC"], "alarm_features": ["Constitutional symptoms (weight loss, fever, night sweats)", "Acute visual changes"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.48428571428571426, "posterior": 0.052667368833697, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["Lack of lymphadenopathy or skin findings reduces the likelihood of lymphoid neoplasms (B2) and supports a myeloid process (B1)."], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.8, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute myeloid leukemia", "myelodysplastic syndrome with excess blasts", "chronic myeloid leukemia in blast crisis"]}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.19261363636363635, "posterior": 0.4898842998530545, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["CNS infiltration is more common in lymphoid neoplasms, but absence of specific lymphoid features (e.g., lymphadenopathy) reduces their likelihood."], "evidence_against": ["Lack of lymphadenopathy or skin findings reduces the likelihood of lymphoid neoplasms (B2) and supports a myeloid process (B1)."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.85, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": ["acute lymphoblastic leukemia", "lymphoblastic lymphoma"]}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase)", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.24214285714285713, "posterior": 0.28436617215279686, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": ["Absence of chronic MPN features (splenomegaly, prolonged fatigue) further reduces their likelihood."], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.6, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "urgency", "representative_diseases": ["chronic myeloid leukemia", "polycythemia vera", "essential thrombocythemia", "primary myelofibrosis"]}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.1375811688311688, "posterior": 0.16157168872317998, "danger": 0.65, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.6, "interrupt_relevance": 0.5, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.012, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Blasts are pathognomonic for malignancy, providing strong exclusion of reactive leukocytosis (B5)."]}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2", "B3", "B4"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the visual changes (20/100 acuity) and ataxia suggest central nervous system infiltration, which is more characteristic of lymphoid neoplasms like ALL than myeloid neoplasms?", "summary": "CNS infiltration is more common in lymphoid neoplasms, but absence of specific lymphoid features (e.g., lymphadenopathy) reduces their likelihood."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the rapid onset (days) and blast percentage (35%) argue against a chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm in stable phase and support blast crisis?", "summary": "The acute presentation and high blasts strongly argue against stable chronic MPN and favor a myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the findings of thrombocytopenia (109,000/mm³) and anemia (Hb 10 g/dL) inconsistent with a chronic myeloproliferative phase and thus support the need for urgent evaluation of myeloid neoplasm?", "summary": "Anemia and thrombocytopenia are more consistent with myeloid neoplasms than chronic MPN in stable phase."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of described lymphadenopathy or skin abnormalities (e.g., acrocyanosis) inconsistent with a lymphoid neoplasm and supportive of a myeloid process?", "summary": "Lack of lymphadenopathy or skin findings reduces the likelihood of lymphoid neoplasms (B2) and supports a myeloid process (B1)."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of described splenomegaly or chronic phase symptoms (e.g., fatigue over months) inconsistent with a diagnosis of chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm?", "summary": "Absence of chronic MPN features (splenomegaly, prolonged fatigue) further reduces their likelihood."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of blasts (35%) definitively rule out non-malignant leukocytosis and justify immediate exclusion of B5?", "summary": "Blasts are pathognomonic for malignancy, providing strong exclusion of reactive leukocytosis (B5)."}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.49], ["B3", 0.284], ["B4", 0.162]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": false, "reason": "No universal instability markers identified", "required_actions": []}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Leading hypothesis (B2) has posterior 0.49 < 0.75; dangerous alternatives (B1: danger 0.9, posterior 0.05) exist but posterior < 0.10. Tree depth (1) < max (3) and remaining diagnostic value (0.8) justifies continued expansion."}, "turn_budget_used": 1, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.8, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.4898842998530545, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Acute myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Chronic myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Leukocytosis'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.49}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "posterior": 0.49, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 1, "disconfirming": 2, "last_disconfirming_digest": "Lack of lymphadenopathy or skin findings reduces the likelihood of lymphoid neoplasms (B2) and supports a myeloid proce…"}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "confirm"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "refute"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B3", "B4", "B1"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=75%, source=primekg]\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts:\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, decreased circulating antibody level\n  Favours second: abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n\nMyeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of the bladder, abnormality of vitamin b12 metabolism, acute kidney injury, bone pain, elevated serum creatinine\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nLymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts vs Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase):\n  Favours first:  abnormality of skin physiology, abnormality of the endocrine system, acrocyanosis, areflexia, arterial thrombosis\n  Favours second: abnormal bronchus physiology, abnormal cd4:cd8 ratio, absent speech, agammaglobulinemia, anorexia\n\nRelated to Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: cd4+/cd56+ hematodermic neoplasm, hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, mast cell neoplasm\n\nRelated to Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts: hematopoietic and lymphoid cell neoplasm, lymphoid leukemia (disease), lymphoma\n\nRelated to Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm (chronic/accelerated phase): bone marrow cancer, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, chronic eosinophilic leukemia, not otherwise specified\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  and night sweats → fatigue → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts\n  and night sweats → pain → Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts"}
