isorders (B4), providing weak evidence against B4 and weak support for B1/B2."]}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.01, "danger": 0.4, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": ["Blasts are pathognomonic for malignancy, providing strong exclusion of reactive leukocytosis (B5)."]}}, "frontier": ["B2"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 2, "type": "SAFETY_ENSURE", "content": "Does the presence of CNS symptoms (ataxia, visual changes) rule out myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts (B1) as the cause of blasts, given that myeloid processes typically don't present with promi…", "summary": "CNS involvement is rare in myeloid neoplasms but more common in lymphoid neoplasms. This finding moderately supports B2 and weakly argues against B1."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Do the CNS symptoms (visual changes, ataxia) and absence of lymphadenopathy support lymphoid neoplasm with increased blasts (B2), despite the subacute presentation?", "summary": "CNS symptoms and lack of lymphadenopathy weakly support B2 but do not overcome the subacute presentation typically seen in lymphoid neoplasms."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the 35% blasts in a patient without chronic MPN features (splenomegaly, prolonged fatigue) represent chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm in accelerated phase (B3)?", "summary": "The absence of chronic MPN features (splenomegaly, prolonged fatigue) provides weak evidence against B3."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the lack of described lymphadenopathy or skin findings inconsistent with lymphoid neoplasm (B2) and supportive of myeloid neoplasm (B1)?", "summary": "Absence of lymphadenopathy weakly supports B1 over B2 but does not definitively distinguish them."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is the absence of splenomegaly or chronic sympt